          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong> Synthetic domain theory
            , as its name suggests, is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+mathematics"} synthetic
            axiomatization of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain+theory"} domain theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/toposes"} toposes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+mathematics"} intuitionistic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            with certain anti-classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            and defines a notion of domain internally. These synthetic domains act like the analytic notions in that domains support arbitrary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+points"} fixed points
            and solutions to non-well-founded recursive domain equations.
          %h2#axiomatics Axiomatics
          %p
            Specific axioms of SDT vary, but a category for synthetic domain theory is typically a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
            equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dominance"} dominance
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+map+classifier"} partial map classifier
            can be constructed called the “lift”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+algebra+of+an+endofunctor"} initial and final algebras
            (as a functor, rather than a monad) called
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline\omega
            respectively, with an induced
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo →
                    %mover
                      %mi ω
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \to \overline \omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mover
                      %mi ω
                      %mo ¯
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma^{\overline \omega} \to \Sigma^\omega
            has an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
          %p
            An intuition is that maps out of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            are a synthetic notion of
            = succeed "-chains" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CPO"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
                \-CPOs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline \omega
            are
            = succeed "-chains" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            has a
            %em unique
            limit point.
          %h2#models Models
          %p
            There are two main classes of models of synthetic domain theory:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/realizability+toposes"} realizability toposes
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+toposes"} Grothendieck toposes
          %p
            A Grothendieck topos model that is very closely related to the analytic notion of
            = succeed "-CPO" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+topology"} canonical topology
            on the full subcategory of
            = succeed "-CPO" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi ω
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline \omega
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega + 1
            = succeed "-CPO" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            = succeed "-CPOs" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            = succeed "-separated" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ¬
                    %mo ¬
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neg\neg
